  "id": "COMPOUND:980 - PROTEIN:5971",
  "verbalization": "Doxorubicin is implicated in the regulation of Lysine-specific demethylase 5C through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that doxorubicin acts as a target of several molecules, which in turn interact with Lysine-specific demethylase 5C. Specifically, doxorubicin is linked to Probable G-protein coupled receptor 132 and P2X purinoceptor 1, both of which are targets of other compounds that ultimately affect Lysine-specific demethylase 5C. These pathways highlight a potential role for doxorubicin in modulating the activity of Lysine-specific demethylase 5C through receptor-mediated signaling. The integration of these relationships underscores a complex network where doxorubicin may influence the enzyme through distinct molecular mechanisms, possibly involving receptor activation or other signaling cascades. The evidence supports a mechanism where doxorubicin indirectly affects Lysine-specific demethylase 5C via its interaction with these target molecules, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
